# Playbook: large task

Planning and carrying out a large task (`AGENTS.md` → `Workflow`). Read it before planning a large task and when one goes on from `handoff.md`.

## Contents
- Planning
- Execution
- Usage limit
- Context refresh
- New session
- Parallel tasks

## Planning

Ask the questions with the `ph-grilling` skill. If it is not installed (a new clone has no skills: they are outside git), install it first with `npx @jiemurat/pan-harness@latest init`. The questions come round by round:
- Question 1 of the first round is a draft of the goal, the outcome and the criteria, which the owner approves or corrects:
  - the goal and what the owner has at the end: 2–3 sentences, plain words, terms explained;
  - criteria: 1–5, each an outcome the owner sees: what is observed, the threshold (a number or yes/no), how and when it is checked; a criterion holds only this task's outcome, the checks of every task are in `playbooks/end-of-task.md`;
  - evidence is re-run where possible (a command, a script, a test);
  - each criterion and each step names its check command, or `no command` and the evidence it uses; a step with a command is `done` only when the command passes or prints the expected output, with that output as evidence;
  - for a subjective outcome, a rubric of 2–4 levels and who grades; for a numeric criterion, an anchor that shows the real goal (the owner's spot check, real use, ground truth); an agent or LLM grader is first compared with the owner's grades on a small sample;
- each round asks every question that can be answered now, in order of impact: the low-impact questions with a clear recommendation go into one "accept as recommended?" list, from which the owner picks out what they want. A question that depends on another answer waits for the next round;
- each question in the form of `playbooks/questions.md` (the `ph-grilling` skill writes it too): options a), b), c), the recommendation and its reason. The numbers run on to the end of the task;
- the answer comes as "1. a 2. c …";
- the rounds go into the task's planning file, `pan-harness/planning/<task>.md` (`<task>`: a short name in lowercase with hyphens). Make it at the first round and link it from the task's `plan.md` → `Queue` item ("planning: `pan-harness/planning/<task>.md`"); the item itself stays one or two lines. The file has a `**Status:** planning` line, a request line, `**Request:** "<the owner's first words, as they are>" (for the why; the approved goal and criteria win where they differ)`, and these sections: `## Goal draft`, `## Criteria draft`, `## Rounds` (each round's questions with the owner's answers, in order), `## Research` (links and short findings), `## Plan` (the short plan below), `## Next question`, and under the status line this paragraph, copied as it is: "A new session: send again only a round without answers, as written in `## Rounds`: the same numbers, options, option letters and `➡️` letters, translated into the chat language when it differs. When the owner's answers come, the first file change, before any other (a Session start fix too), writes them all into `## Rounds`, each with its question, in one edit. The status line, the plan and the approval: `playbooks/large-task.md` → `Planning`." Before you send a round, write it into `## Rounds` as you send it — each question with its options and its `➡️` line with the reason, in the file's language — and its first question into `## Next question`; commit after each answer.
- at the end, a short plan: the final list of criteria, which files of the project change (the harness files every task updates are not listed), the exceptions, the budget (time, tokens or the number of sub-agents, paid API cost), where the owner's hand is needed. Work that does not fit one session is split into stages, each ending with its own check and report. [profile: live-system=yes] Restarts and outages are named too. The approval covers the criteria as well: after it, the goal, the criteria and the exceptions go into the task's item in `plan.md`, the stages and steps into `pan-harness/handoff.md` (`**Status:** active`). Before you give the plan, write it into the planning file's `## Plan`, set its status line to `**Status:** awaiting approval` and commit. On the approval, the first file change is `**Status:** approved` in the planning file; then move the planning file: the goal, the criteria and the exceptions into the task's `plan.md` item, the request line into `Task` of `handoff.md`, the answers and decisions into its `Decisions` section, the research links into its `Work files`; then remove the file (`git rm`). Then `## New session`, signal 2: open a sub-session for the work, or without session tools tell the owner to open a new session in this folder and write "go on"; write no code in this session. When `handoff.md` already says `**Status:** active` (another large task), ask the owner first: wait for that task to end (set the planning file's status line to `**Status:** approved, waiting` and keep the file) or run the two at once (`## Parallel tasks` below). Before the approval, only reading is allowed, with one exception: the task's planning file and the link to it in `plan.md` are written and committed after each round of answers.
- step 1 of every plan retitles the session to the task with the `ph-rename-claude-session` skill (its short form), unless the owner said not to (`feedback.md`); with no session tools, the step is marked done as "no session tools". A worker-agent leaves this step out.
- A session whose brief says "- Umbrella plan: approved" takes the goal, the criteria and the decisions from the brief and asks only what they do not answer, all in one round. With no such question, the umbrella approval covers its plan: write `handoff.md` (`**Status:** active`) and start. With questions, keep them in the task's planning file, as in any planning: the brief's task line as its request line, the brief's goal and criteria in its drafts, the brief's decisions and the round in `## Rounds`; `handoff.md` stays untouched. After the answers, give the plan from `## Plan` (`**Status:** awaiting approval`) and wait for the owner's word.

## Execution

- At the end of each stage, write its history entry (`playbooks/end-of-task.md` → `End of task`) before the stage commit, then shorten the stage in `handoff.md`: each of its steps keeps one line, "`done` — history <date>: <outcome>", and its evidence and checks stay only in the history entry. Done when `handoff.md` holds no evidence of a finished stage.
- When a step in `handoff.md` is `done`, write the owner a one-sentence status message and update `handoff.md`: the step done with its evidence, the reason of a decision, the next step.
- Before each stop (a question to the owner, a wait for the owner's word, a report), update `handoff.md`: `Changed files`, `Open questions`, `Next step`. While the task waits for the owner's answer to a question in `Open questions`, the status line says `waiting-owner`; the edit that moves the answer into `Decisions` sets it back to `active`. When the owner's answer comes, the first file change moves it from `Open questions` into `Decisions` of `handoff.md` in one edit; then go on. The file on disk is enough for a new session; the commit stays at the stage end. At such a stop, and at a stop of the planning once the planning file is written, the context offer of `playbooks/end-of-task.md` → `Context offer` applies.
- Run the checks in full even near the end of the context: if time runs out, update the handoff, and the work goes on in the next session.
- If the budget doubles, stop and ask the owner.
- Check each criterion with evidence: if it can be reached within the plan, fix and go on; if it needs leaving the plan or the criterion turns out wrong, stop and ask the owner. Only the owner changes a criterion.
- The task is done when its own checks pass, there is no new error and each criterion is ✅ or ⏳.

## Usage limit

For every task, not only a large one, and for the agents of the sub-sessions and sub-agents it starts (`AGENTS.md` → `Working style` → "Usage limit"):
- A bigger step: each `handoff.md` step, each sub-agent or sub-session start, each test, build or behaviour-test run, and any step you expect to take over 20 tool calls.
- Before a bigger step, read the 5-hour usage limit when your agent has a tool that shows it (Claude Desktop: `mcp__ccd_session_mgmt__get_usage` → `plan.windows`, the "5-hour limit" row, `percentUsed`). Done when the share used is known, or no such tool is there.
- At 90% used or more: write `pan-harness/handoff.md` with `**Status:** active` (for a small task too: Task, Changed files, Next step), leave the files in the work tree and commit only when the checks pass. Then send the owner one message in the chat language: the share left and its reset time, that the work is saved in `handoff.md` and another agent can go on from it in this folder, and the question whether to wait or go on; end the turn. Done when the message is sent.
- On "go on", go on and ask again only after the reset time. On "wait", the turn stays ended; the owner's later "go on" resumes from `handoff.md`.
- A sub-agent cannot talk to the owner: at 90% used or more it stops and hands back what is done, the changed files and the next step; the agent that started it writes `handoff.md` and asks the owner as above.
- The brief of a sub-agent carries the usage-limit line of `playbooks/delegation.md` → "The brief". The brief of a session started in another folder, or of a worker-agent, carries: "- Usage limit: before each bigger step read the 5-hour limit; at under 10% left, write `pan-harness/handoff.md` of this folder, stop, tell the owner another agent can go on from it, and ask whether to wait or go on."
- If you can see the usage limit, before a large task, a sub-agent run or a stage over 10% of the 5-hour window, compare the budget with the share left and its renewal time. Base the budget on the last similar stage (`handoff.md`: "62% → 55%"); with no measurement, put the stage off when under 10% is left.
- If the budget is over, the scope stays: start the part that fits, put the rest into `plan.md` → `Scheduled` at the renewal time, tell the owner in one sentence.
- If you cannot see the limit, go on without asking: write "limit: not checked" on the budget line of the plan and of `handoff.md`.

## Context refresh

For a session the main-agent started (a worker-agent, a part session, any other sub-session) and for the main-agent, in an agent with the session tools (Claude Desktop: `mcp__ccd_session_mgmt__get_usage`, `mcp__ccd_session_mgmt__clear_session`, `SendMessage`). A clear empties the conversation; the work goes on from the files.

**The brief file.** Before it starts a sub-session, the main-agent writes the brief into the brief file beside the sub-session's state file: `pan-harness/brief.md`, or `pan-harness/brief-<task>.md` beside `handoff-<task>.md`. It is read only on a refresh and removed with the state file at the task's end. The brief carries the refresh line: "- Context refresh: brief in <brief file>. Before each bigger step read your context size; past 500 thousand tokens, check that your state file is current, leave no timer or background task running, and end the turn with a message whose first line is 'Context refresh: <N>K, state in <state file>, brief in <brief file>, ready to clear.' On 'Cleared for a context refresh', read the brief and state files and go on."

The sub-session:
- Before each bigger step (`## Usage limit`), the same `get_usage` call gives `context.tokensUsed`. Done when the size is known.
- Past 500 thousand tokens: check that the state file (`pan-harness/handoff.md`, or `handoff-<task>.md`; for a small task write it with `**Status:** active`: Task, Changed files, Next step) matches `git status`, and that an open question to the owner is in its `Open questions`. Leave no timer or background task running: the app refuses to clear a session with live work (a scheduled job counts). End the turn with a message whose first line is "Context refresh: <N>K, state in <state file>, brief in <brief file>, ready to clear.": the main-agent's note quotes only the start of it. Done when the line is sent; the session then waits for the main-agent, whose message comes once the main-agent is idle.
- On "Cleared for a context refresh": read the brief file and the state file and go on from `Next step`. An open question to the owner is sent again unchanged.
- On "Not cleared": go on, and stop for the refresh next only past each next 100 thousand (600, 700, …).

The main-agent, on a sub-session's note that holds the "Context refresh" line (a note cut short without it, "first 400 of N characters": read the session's last message, `list_events`, for the line):
- Check that the state file it names has a `Next step`. Clear the session (`clear_session` with its id) and send "Cleared for a context refresh: read <brief file> and <state file>, go on from `Next step`." Tell the owner in one line: "<name>: context <N>K, cleared, going on." Done when the message is sent.
- A refused clear (the session is on the owner's screen, pinned, holds live work, or the permission is denied): send "Not cleared: go on from `Next step`." and tell the owner in one line why.
- Clearing and sending work on a turn a session's note started; starting a new session there does not. In the default permission mode each clear asks the owner; in auto mode it runs on its own. A model whose window is under 500 thousand tokens relies on its own auto-compaction.

## New session

For every task, in an agent with the session tools (Claude Desktop: `mcp__ccd_session__start_session`, `SendMessage`, `mcp__ccd_session_mgmt__archive_session`, `mcp__ccd_session_mgmt__delete_session`). A sub-session takes over a unit of work in a fresh context in this folder; its opener waits and coordinates. Worker-agents (`ph-workers`) and part sessions keep their own ways; `playbooks/end-of-task.md` → `Context offer` and `## Context refresh` stay.

**The signals.**
1. The same failure after two fixes: the same check still fails after the second fix (yours or one the state file records), or the owner corrects the same thing a second time. Write the attempts and your current guess into the state file; then give a fresh read-only sub-agent the failing check and the attempts (`playbooks/delegation.md`), and make no third fix before its answer. When your context is over 100 thousand tokens too, open a sub-session instead.
2. A large task's plan is approved: after the move of `## Planning`, open a sub-session for the work; write no code in this session.
3. The next unit of work (the next task or stage) needs little of this conversation, its state is in files, and your context is over 100 thousand tokens.

**Opening one.** Done when the child runs and the owner has the line.
- Your own work needs a state file with a Work ID: a small task writes a short `pan-harness/handoff-<task>.md` first (`**Status:** active`, Task, Changed files, Next step).
- Make the child's Work ID: `node pan-harness/scripts/pan-harness-check.mjs --new-id <child-task>`. Write its state file `pan-harness/handoff-<child-task>.md` (the sections of `handoff.md`) with `**Work ID:**` and `**Opened by:** <your Work ID>`, and its brief file `brief-<child-task>.md` (`## Context refresh` → "The brief file"), which adds: "- Level: <your level + 1>" and "- When done: message your opener, session <your session id>: 'Done: <Work ID>, <commit>'."
- A child that writes files lists them before it starts (`## Parallel tasks`); a child that only reads (a search, a review) runs beside others freely.
- Commit the two files, open the sub-session with the brief's text, and tell the owner one line: the signal, the session's link, "the work goes on there".
- At most 4 levels below the owner's session: a child at `Level: 4` opens no sub-session and works on itself.

**In the sub-session.** The owner answers its questions in its own session. When its work is done (its checks pass, its commit made), it messages its opener as the brief says and waits.

**The opener, on "Done".** Check the result against the task's goal and criteria, not only the child's word (its diff and checks, `playbooks/delegation.md` → `The result`); remove the child's state and brief files, commit, report up (to the owner, or "Done" to your own opener), and archive the child at once (`archive_session`). At the task's end, delete all the task's archived children in one approval card (`delete_session` with their ids from the app's session list). Done when no child of the task is left.

**Without session tools.** At a signal, write the state file and add one sentence: the work goes better in a new session; open one in this folder and write "go on".

**A stopped tree.** With several open state files and no task named, `--tree` (Session start) prints them as a tree and one `Action:` line: go on with the root, and open sessions again for its open children, each from its own brief and state file; each does the same for its children. Without session tools, show the owner the tree and ask them to open a session in this folder for each open child, its first message naming the child's state file.

## Parallel tasks

A second large task runs while another is active in one of two ways; the owner picks one on the approval (`## Planning`):
- **A worker-agent** (`ph-workers`): the task runs in the worker's own folder and branch, with its own `handoff.md` there.
- **A second session in this folder**: the task's state goes into `pan-harness/handoff-<task>.md` (the sections of `handoff.md`), made from the planning file and kept to the task's end, also when `handoff.md` is freed; at the end it is removed. Before it starts, each plan lists the files its task changes and the checks it runs. When the lists share a file, or one task's check covers the other's files (for example both change the artifact-folder and the fast check tests all of it), tell the owner the risk — one task's check fails on the other's unfinished work, and the hook stops its commits — and ask: a worker-agent in its own folder, wait for the other task, or this folder at the owner's risk.

With two or more tasks active in one folder (the sub-sessions of `## New session` too):
- Commit only your task's files: `git commit -m "<message>" -- <files>` (another session may have staged its own).
- The `Changed files` of every active state file (`handoff.md`, `handoff-*.md`) are not the owner's changes.
- Before you edit a shared journal (`plan.md`, `state.md`, `decisions.md`, the history), read it again and change only your task's lines; the history gets only new entries; `state.md` → `In progress` names each task.
- When the hook stops your commit on the other task's file, name the file and its task to the owner, then wait or ask; `--no-verify` only on the owner's word.
- A `git commit` that fails on `index.lock: File exists` (another session is committing): wait 5 seconds and run it again, up to 3 times; never delete the lock file; then tell the owner.
- [profile: live-system=yes] Each plan lists the live parts its task changes (services, config files). Before a restart or deploy, look at the other running tasks (`pan-harness/workers.md`, the active state files); when they touch the same parts, ask the owner for the time and the order. A config file both tasks change reaches the live system only after the merge.
